Everything in this repository is MIT-licensed as above, EXCEPT
`crates/ferx-search` and `data/mbma_naproxen.csv`, described below.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Directory exception: `crates/ferx-search`

Everything under `crates/ferx-search` -- its sources, tests and test fixtures
-- is licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License, version 3 or (at
your option) any later version (LGPL-3.0-or-later), not under the MIT grant
above. See `crates/ferx-search/NOTICE`, `crates/ferx-search/COPYING.LESSER`
(LGPL-3.0) and `crates/ferx-search/COPYING` (GPL-3.0). This is not
retroactive: the model-search code was MIT-licensed in earlier ferx-tools
releases, up to and including ferx-tools 0.4.0, and those releases remain MIT
(`crates/ferx-search/LICENSE-MIT`).

The rest of the repository stays MIT, including:

  - ferx-core (the root package, `src/`), `crates/ferx-tools` and
    `crates/ferx-cli`. Only ferx-cli depends on ferx-search, and its own
    source is MIT; the compiled `ferx` binary links ferx-search and so
    carries the LGPL notices (`ferx --licenses`).
  - `tools/pharmpy-*`, the scripts that run Pharmpy in a container to produce
    the anchor fixtures under `crates/ferx-search/tests/data/`. They call
    Pharmpy as a program and contain none of its code.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Per-file exception: `data/mbma_naproxen.csv`

`data/mbma_naproxen.csv` is not MIT-licensed either. That test fixture is
derived from an open access publication licensed CC BY-NC 4.0 (Boucher M,
Bennetts M, CPT:PSP 2018;7:288-297, doi:10.1002/psp4.12299) and carries that
licence, not this one -- so commercial use of that file is not granted. See
`data/mbma_naproxen.LICENSE` for the terms and `data/mbma_naproxen.README.md`
for the provenance. It is test data only: it is never linked into the `ferx`
binary, and `Cargo.toml` excludes the whole `data/mbma_naproxen.*` group from
the packaged crate, so the crate published to crates.io carries no CC BY-NC
file at all.

The two companion files caught by that exclude glob are not the article's and
are not CC BY-NC: `data/mbma_naproxen.README.md` is repo-authored prose under
the MIT grant above, and `data/mbma_naproxen.LICENSE` reproduces Creative
Commons' own licence text, which CC does not license under itself. They are
excluded from the crate because they are meaningless without the data file,
not because of their licence.
